course: books
lesson: 70
title: The Wind in the Willows, chapter XII, part 1: The Return of Ulysses (dressing for battle, the dark passage and «Good Toad, modest Toad»)
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: At dusk the Rat dresses everybody for the expedition: a belt, a sword, a cutlass, a pair of pistols, a policeman's truncheon, handcuffs, bandages, a flask and a sandwich-case each. Badger laughs that he'll do everything with his stick, but lets the Rat have his way. Then they set off in a line: Badger with a dark lantern, then Mole («'cos I'm very pleased with him»), then Rat, and Toad last — with strict orders not to chatter.
explain: They slip into a hole in the river-bank. Of course Toad falls into the water with a splash and a squeal. In the cold, dark, narrow passage he lags behind, gets frightened, rushes forward and knocks Rat into Mole and Mole into Badger. Badger thinks they are being attacked from behind and nearly fires his pistol! After that the Rat walks at the back holding Toad firmly by the shoulder.
explain: At last, right above their heads, they hear cheering, stamping and glasses clinking — the weasels' party. They come to a trap-door, push it up together and climb into the butler's pantry. Only a door is between them and the banqueting-hall.
explain: Through the door they hear the Chief Weasel making a speech about «our kind host, Mr. Toad... Good Toad, modest Toad, honest Toad!» while everyone shrieks with laughter. Toad grinds his teeth. The Chief Weasel begins a song — «Toad he went a-pleasuring / Gaily down the street—» — and Badger grips his stick with both paws: «The hour is come! Follow me!» He flings the door open wide.
fact: The chapter's title, «The Return of Ulysses», points to an old Greek story: the hero Ulysses (Odysseus) came home after years of adventures, found strangers feasting in his house, and drove them out — just as Toad is about to do.
fact: Each animal got «a belt... and then a sword... and then a cutlass on the other side to balance it. Then a pair of pistols, a policeman's truncheon, several sets of handcuffs, some bandages and sticking-plaster, and a flask and a sandwich-case.» (a truncheon = a policeman's short club)
fact: Badger: «All right, Ratty! It amuses you and it doesn't hurt me. I'm going to do all I've got to do with this here stick.»
fact: The Rat: «You know I shouldn't like you to blame me afterwards and say I had forgotten anything!»
fact: Badger carried «a dark lantern» (a lantern with a cover to hide the light) and set the order: «Mole first, 'cos I'm very pleased with him; Rat next; Toad last.»
fact: «Don't you chatter so much as usual, or you'll be sent back, as sure as fate!» Toad took his place «without a murmur», so anxious was he not to be left out.
fact: Getting into the hole in the river-bank, Toad «managed to slip and fall into the water with a loud splash and a squeal of alarm»; Badger warned that next time he would be left behind.
fact: The passage was «cold, and dark, and damp, and low, and narrow»; Toad began to shiver, «partly from dread of what might be before him, partly because he was wet through», lagged behind in the dark, then rushed forward so hard «that he upset the Rat into the Mole and the Mole into the Badger».
fact: Badger thought they were attacked from behind and «drew a pistol, and was on the point of putting a bullet into Toad» — but stopped when he saw what had happened.
fact: The others promised to be «answerable for his good conduct» (to promise he would behave), and the Rat walked last, «with a firm grip on the shoulder of Toad».
fact: Hearing the noise above, Badger said placidly (calmly): «They are going it, the Weasels!» (going it = having a wild time)
fact: The passage ended under «the trap-door that led up into the butler's pantry»; all four put their shoulders to it and heaved it back.
fact: The Chief Weasel's speech: «I should like to say one word about our kind host, Mr. Toad. We all know Toad!»—(great laughter)—«Good Toad, modest Toad, honest Toad!» (shrieks of merriment). This is sarcasm: saying the opposite of what you mean, to mock someone.
fact: The weasel's song begins «Toad he went a-pleasuring / Gaily down the street—» (a-pleasuring = going out for fun); Toad mutters «Only just let me get at him!»
fact: Badger: «The hour is come! Follow me!» — and flung the door open wide. In chapter VI Badger had said almost the same: «The hour has come!... Toad's hour!»
fact: Talk in this lesson: «bare» (London) = lots of; «hold up» = wait; «wicked» = great; «arvo» (Australia) = afternoon; «nesh» (North and Midlands England) = feels the cold easily.
exercise: Who dressed the animals up for the expedition? | The Rat | Old Badger | Mr. Toad | Mole, in the blue boudoir
exercise: What was put on each sword's other side «to balance it»? | A cutlass | A pistol | A truncheon | A sandwich-case full of ham sandwiches
exercise: Which of these did the Rat NOT give them? | A motor-car horn | Handcuffs | Bandages | A flask of tea
exercise: What did Badger say he would do everything with? | His stick | His big sword | His pistols | His bare paws and his big strong teeth
exercise: Why did the Rat insist on all the equipment? | So nobody could blame him for forgetting anything | He liked shopping | He was afraid of the dark and wanted to feel safe | Badger told him to
exercise: What did Badger carry besides his stick? | A dark lantern | A map | A flag | A bag of buns
exercise: Who went first behind Badger? | Mole | The Rat | Mr. Toad | Otter, who knew the way
exercise: Why did Mole go first? | Badger was very pleased with him | He was the biggest | He had dug the passage himself | He had the lantern
exercise: Where did Toad have to go in the line? | Last | First | Second | In front of Badger, to show the way to his own house
exercise: What warning did Badger give Toad? | Don't chatter, or you'll be sent back | Don't sing | Don't eat | Don't fall asleep in the tunnel
exercise: Where was the entrance to the secret passage? | A hole in the river-bank, a little above the water | Under a tree | Behind the Rat's fireplace | In the boat-house
exercise: What happened when it was Toad's turn to get into the hole? | He slipped and fell in the water with a splash | He got stuck halfway because of all his weapons | He refused | He went in first
exercise: Which word does NOT describe the passage? | Warm | Very cold | Damp and dripping | Narrow
exercise: Why did Toad rush forward in the dark? | He was terrified of being left behind alone | He saw a light | He heard music | He wanted to go first
exercise: What did Toad's rush do? | Knocked the Rat into Mole and Mole into Badger | Put out the lantern and left them all in the dark | Woke the weasels | Broke his sword
exercise: What did Badger think was happening? | They were being attacked from behind | Toad had fallen into a deep hole | The roof of the passage was falling in on them | The weasels had found them
exercise: Who walked at the back after that, holding Toad? | The Rat | The Mole | Old Badger | Otter, who had come along to help
exercise: «They are going it, the Weasels!» What does «going it» mean? | Having a wild time | Going away | Going to sleep | Going out to fight
exercise: What did they hear above their heads? | Cheering, stamping and glasses clinking | Snoring | Rain pouring down on the roof of Toad Hall | Singing of hymns
exercise: Where did the trap-door lead? | Into the butler's pantry | Into the wine cellar | Into Toad's own bedroom | Into the garden
exercise: How did they open the trap-door? | All four heaved it up together | Badger used a big iron key | Rat shot the lock off with his pistol | Toad knocked
exercise: «Our kind host, Mr. Toad.» Who was making this speech? | The Chief Weasel | The Sergeant | A ferret | A tipsy stoat
exercise: «Good Toad, modest Toad, honest Toad!» What kind of speaking is this? | Sarcasm | Real praise for a kind host | A prayer | A riddle
exercise: What did Toad mutter when he heard the speech? | «Only just let me get at him!» | «How kind of them to remember me!» | «Poop-poop!» | «Let's go home»
exercise: What did Badger cry as he flung the door open? | «The hour is come! Follow me!» | «Charge!» | «Hands up, all of you, you are surrounded!» | «Learn 'em!»
test: Olly, bare (lots of) confused: «so Badger loaded up with all the Rat's weapons?» Put it right. | He said he'd use only his stick | Yes, he used every one of them in the fight | He carried nothing | He carried the Rat's pistols only
test: Why did Badger put Toad last in the line? | To keep him out of trouble | Toad was the slowest walker of the four | Toad had the lantern | Toad wanted it
test: Is it TRUE or FALSE? «Toad got into the passage quietly and neatly.» | False: he fell in the river | True, as quietly and neatly as a mouse | True: Badger carried him | False: he never got in
test: Nell, nesh (feels the cold): «why was Toad shivering, scared or cold?» What does the book say? | Both: dread and wet clothes | Only scared of the weasels | Only cold | Neither, he was laughing
test: What would have happened if Badger had fired his pistol in the passage? | He might have hurt Toad and warned the weasels | Nothing | The trap-door would have flown open by itself | The weasels would have cheered
test: Why could the friends climb into the pantry without being heard? | The party was deafening | The weasels were all asleep after the feast | The door was locked | The pantry was far away
test: Why do the weasels laugh at «honest Toad»? | Toad was jailed for stealing a car | Toad really is honest and they admire him | They like Toad | They didn't hear
test: Kai, wicked (great) timing: «why did Badger wait for the song to start?» Best answer? | The weasels were busy and least ready | Badger liked the tune and wanted to hear it | Badger forgot | He was eating
test: Looking back: in chapter VI, at the Rat's house, what did Badger say about «the hour»? | «The hour has come!... Toad's hour!» | «The hour of tea, with hot buttered toast» | «Bedtime» | «The hour of the weasels»
test: Looking back: in chapter III, what did the Rat take into the Wild Wood to find Mole? | A brace of pistols and a stout cudgel | A boat | A lantern only, because he never carried weapons | A map and compass
test: Looking back: in chapter IV, how did Badger take Rat and Mole out of his home to the edge of the wood? | Through a long tunnel | Over the snow, on top of the drifts | By boat | On a cart
test: Looking back: in chapter II, how did the Rat behave about Toad's caravan plan? | He went only to please his friends | He was thrilled and planned every day of it | He drove | He bought the horse
test: Looking back: in chapter XI, why did Badger want the attack during the banquet? | The weasels would have no weapons | They would be asleep after the party | It was raining | Toad was at home
test: Looking back: in chapter XI, why was Badger «very pleased» with Mole? | Mole's trick had scattered the sentries | Mole cooked a fine lunch | Mole carried the guns all the way from the river | Mole found the passage
test: Why is «The Return of Ulysses» a clever title for this chapter? | Like Ulysses, Toad comes home to find enemies feasting in his house | Toad visits Greece | Ulysses is the name of the Chief Weasel | It's about a boat called Ulysses
test: ★★★ Count the items the Rat gave each animal, from «a belt» to «a sandwich-case» (count «bandages and sticking-plaster» as two, and «a pair of pistols» as one). | 10 | 8 | 9 | 12
test: ★★★ «He upset the Rat into the Mole and the Mole into the Badger.» Who was at the front of the line? | Badger | Mole | Rat | Toad
test: ★★★ «Good Toad, modest Toad, honest Toad!» Which word would the weasels say honestly about Toad, judging from the story so far? | None of them | Good | Modest, because he hid in prison | Honest
exam: Identify the instrument with which the Badger declared he would accomplish his share of the enterprise. | His stick | A pair of pistols | A sharp cutlass | A policeman's truncheon
exam: Ascertain the order of march prescribed by the Badger, exclusive of himself. | Mole, then Rat, then Toad | Toad, then Rat, then Mole | Rat, then Toad, then Mole | Rat, then Mole, then Toad
exam: It is given that Toad precipitated a collision within the passage. State the misapprehension thereby occasioned in the Badger. | That the party was being attacked from the rear | That the roof of the passage had collapsed upon them | That Toad had deserted | That the weasels had discovered them
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the Chief Weasel's commendation of «Good Toad, modest Toad, honest Toad»? | It was sarcastic, intended to excite derision | It was sincere praise of an absent host | It was a formal toast to the King and the Royal Family | It was a quotation from Scripture
